To create sortable Bootstrap tables without using jQuery or plugins, you can use plain JavaScript. Here's a simple example of how you can achieve this:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Sortable Bootstrap Table</title>
  <link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.0-alpha1/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css" rel="stylesheet" integrity="sha384-KyZXEAg3QhqLMpG8r+Knujsl7/1L_dstPt3HV70md2tm1DtDC+pxF9/Yj4TZvG+z" crossorigin="anonymous">
</head>
<body>
  <table class="table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th onclick="sortTable(0)">Name</th>
        <th onclick="sortTable(1)">Age</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody id="myTable">
      <tr>
        <td>John Doe</td>
        <td>30</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Jane Smith</td>
        <td>28</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Mike Johnson</td>
        <td>25</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <script>
    function sortTable(n) {
      let table, rows, switching, i, x, y, shouldSwitch, dir, switchcount = 0;
      table = document.getElementById("myTable");
      switching = true;
      dir = "asc";
      while (switching) {
        switching = false;
        rows = table.rows;
        for (i = 1; i < (rows.length - 1); i++) {
          shouldSwitch = false;
          x = rows[i].getElementsByTagName("TD")[n];
          y = rows[i + 1].getElementsByTagName("TD")[n];
          if (dir == "asc") {
            if (x.innerHTML.toLowerCase() > y.innerHTML.toLowerCase()) {
              shouldSwitch = true;
              break;
            }
          } else if (dir == "desc") {
            if (x.innerHTML.toLowerCase() < y.innerHTML.toLowerCase()) {
              shouldSwitch = true;
              break;
            }
          }
        }
        if (shouldSwitch) {
          rows[i].parentNode.insertBefore(rows[i + 1], rows[i]);
          switching = true;
          switchcount ++;
        }
      }
      if (switchcount == 0 && dir == "asc") {
        dir = "desc";
        sortTable(n);
      }
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, we have a simple Bootstrap table with two columns: Name and Age. The table headers have onclick events that call the `sortTable()` function, passing the column index as an argument. The `sortTable()` function sorts the table rows based on the clicked column.

The sorting algorithm is a simple bubble sort, which is not the most efficient for large datasets, but it works well for small tables like this one. If you have a larger table or need better performance, you might want to consider using a more efficient sorting algorithm or a library like `sorttable.js`.

