Here's a JavaScript implementation for packing and unpacking an array of entities into an ArrayBuffer. The entities have the fields `id` (4 bits) and `size` (3 bits).

```javascript
function packEntities(entities) {
  const buffer = new ArrayBuffer(entities.length);
  const view = new DataView(buffer);

  for (let i = 0; i < entities.length; i++) {
    const entity = entities[i];
    const id = entity.id;
    const size = entity.size;

    // Pack 4 bits of id into the current position
    view.setUint8(i, id >> 4);
    view.setUint8(i + 1, id & 0xF);

    // Pack 3 bits of size into the current position
    view.setUint8(i + 2, size >> 5);
    view.setUint8(i + 3, (size & 0x1F) << 3);

    // Move to the next entity's position
    i += 4;
  }

  return buffer;
}

function unpackEntities(buffer, length) {
  const view = new DataView(buffer);
  const entities = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < length; i += 4) {
    const id = view.getUint8(i) << 4 | view.getUint8(i + 1);
    const size = view.getUint8(i + 2) >> 3 | (view.getUint8(i + 3) & 0xF) << 5;

    entities.push({ id, size });
  }

  return entities;
}
```

In the `packEntities` function, we create an ArrayBuffer and a DataView to manipulate the buffer. We iterate through the entities and pack their id and size into the buffer. The id is packed into two bytes, and the size is packed into two bytes as well.

In the `unpackEntities` function, we create a DataView and an empty array to store the unpacked entities. We iterate through the buffer in chunks of 4 bytes (for id and size) and unpack the id and size from the buffer. The id is unpacked from two bytes, and the size is unpacked from two bytes as well.

