Demand Paging is a memory management technique used in operating systems where pages are brought into the main memory only when they are required by the CPU, rather than loading the entire program into memory before execution. This technique helps in reducing the memory usage and improves the system performance.

For example, consider a simple program that calculates the factorial of a number. The program starts with the following code:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

unsigned long long factorial(unsigned long long n) {
    if (n <= 1)
        return 1;
    else
        return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

int main() {
    unsigned long long n;
    printf("Enter a number: ");
    scanf("%llu", &n);
    printf("Factorial of %llu is %llu\n", n, factorial(n));
    return 0;
}
```

When this program is executed, the operating system loads only the necessary pages (code, data, and stack) into the main memory. As the program execution proceeds, the CPU requests for the pages containing the function `factorial()` and its variables. The operating system then brings those pages into the main memory using demand paging. This way, only the required pages are loaded into the memory, saving memory space and improving system performance.

